  <h2 id="fit">Matching a course to your stream and constraints</h2>

  <p>
    Most "high salary courses" lists quietly assume you have PCM marks and an unlimited private-college
    budget. Match the realistic version to your actual stream and constraints instead.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Science (PCM/PCB)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>PCM: BTech (via JEE), BArch (via NATA/JEE Paper 2) are the direct-fit routes.</li>
        <li>PCB: nursing and allied health degrees use your biology base directly; BArch and BTech generally require PCM instead.</li>
        <li>Both PCM and PCB students remain eligible for BBA, BCA, BA LLB, BDes, BHM, and the CA route without restriction.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Commerce</div>
      <ul>
        <li>BCom-to-CA, BBA, and BA LLB map directly onto a commerce background.</li>
        <li>BCA and BDes remain open regardless of stream, subject to each institute's specific eligibility rules.</li>
        <li>Engineering and architecture routes are usually closed without a PCM background; check the specific college's eligibility list before assuming otherwise.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Arts / Humanities</div>
      <ul>
        <li>BA LLB, BDes, BHM, and BBA are strong, genuinely open fits.</li>
        <li>BTech, BArch, and nursing degrees are generally not open to pure arts students without the required science subjects; confirm the exact eligibility on the specific institute's current admission notice.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Eligibility rules vary by state, board, and institute. Always confirm the exact subject requirement
    on the specific college, exam body, or council's current admission notice before ruling a course in
    or out based on your stream alone.
  </p>

